  "query_preview": "..\n\nAll hydro related headlines have been dropped as main low and \ndeformation has exited east. A few locations along the western Lake \nErie Shoreline saw rainfall in excess of 2 inches, fortunately \nthunderstorms remained offshore. The upper level wave and mid level \ncold pool (-12 to -13 C at 500 MB) tracking through northern Lake \nHuron is also triggering limited shower activity which will have to \nbe accounted for. An isolated thunderstorm remains possible as well \nwith the decent surface based capes noted in SPC mesoscale analysis \n(especially western Lower Michigan). Activity should quickly \ndissipate toward sunset. \n\nA pleasantly warm and dry start to the workweek is expected as an \nupper-level ridge builds east, with the axis arriving Tuesday \nmorning over the central Great Lakes. \n\nA notable shortwave/upper-level wave currently tracking through \nWashington will move through northern Ontario and into James Bay. \nThus, limited height falls are expected over southern Lower Michigan \nfor the midweek period. A surface cold front will be positioned \nnear/just northwest of the CWA a and will service as a focus for \nconvection. The timing of this frontal passage and the \nactivation/response of showers and possible thunderstorms vary \namongst medium-range models. Thursday appears to be the favored day \nfor best coverage of activity. Euro ensemble suggests 24-hour \nprobability of a tenth of an inch or more of QPF is 60+ percent, \ncentered in the 06Z Thursday to 06Z Friday timeframe. \n\nA broad/loosely defined upper-level trough axis will be in place on \nFriday, persisting into Saturday. However, troughing looks to be \nweak, with pseudo-zonal upper-level flow established over the \nnorthern CONUS over the weekend. With strong high pressure in place \nover the central Appalachians extending into southeast Michigan, we \nexpect mainly dry conditions on Friday and Saturday.",
